# Lessons 1-9. Each card: (term, meaning, exam tip or example)
L = []

L.append(dict(id="statements", title="The financial statements", area="Financial Analysis",
 pis=["FI:579","FI:085","FI:091","FI:093","FI:094","FI:630","FI:354"],
 why="Statement questions show up on every exam, and they're the base for most roleplay cases.",
 cards=[
 ("Need for financial information","Owners and managers use it to track performance, plan and budget, choose investments, meet legal requirements and earn the trust of lenders and investors.","If a question describes comparing forecast (planned) results with actual results, the answer is \"variance.\""),
 ("Accounting","Recording, classifying, summarizing and interpreting a business's financial transactions.","Accounting keeps the records. Finance decides what to do with the money."),
 ("Role of finance in business","Getting money (financing), putting it to work (investing in assets) and managing day-to-day money (working capital management).","The finance function manages working capital and makes capital budgeting decisions."),
 ("Income statement","Shows revenue, expenses and net income (profit or loss) over a period of time. Also called the profit and loss statement (P&L).","Lines in order: sales, cost of goods sold, gross profit, operating expenses, net profit."),
 ("Gross profit","Revenue minus cost of goods sold.","Gross profit comes before operating expenses. Net profit comes after them."),
 ("Balance sheet","A snapshot of what a business owns (assets), owes (liabilities) and is worth to its owners (equity) on one specific date.","Prepared to show the business's current financial situation. Assets = Liabilities + Owner's equity."),
 ("Statement of cash flows","Shows cash coming in and going out during a period, split into operating, investing and financing activities.","Cash in: customer payments, loans received, owner investment, interest received, sale of assets. Cash out: supplier, wage and other expense payments, equipment purchases, loan repayments, dividends."),
 ("Operating activities","Cash from the main business: customer payments in; supplier, wage and tax payments out.","Healthy companies get most of their cash from operations, not from selling assets."),
 ("Investing activities","Buying or selling long-term assets like equipment and buildings, and buying or selling investments.","Buying a delivery truck = cash used in investing."),
 ("Financing activities","Borrowing or repaying loans, owners putting money in or taking it out, issuing stock, paying dividends.","Paying a dividend = financing outflow."),
 ("Statement of changes in equity","Shows how owners' equity changed over a period: starting equity, plus net income and new investment, minus withdrawals or dividends, equals ending equity.","Columns usually include contributed capital, retained earnings and dividends."),
 ("Retained earnings","Profits the company has earned and kept instead of paying out as dividends.","Net income increases retained earnings. Dividends reduce them."),
 ("Dividends","Payments of profit to shareholders.","On the equity statement they're recorded under Dividends and reduce retained earnings."),
 ("Accrual basis","Records revenue when earned and expenses when incurred, whether or not cash has moved. Required by GAAP.","Cash basis records only when cash changes hands."),
 ("GAAP","Generally Accepted Accounting Principles: the US accounting rules, set by the FASB.","IFRS is the international version."),
 ("Current vs fixed assets","Current assets turn into cash within a year (cash, receivables, inventory). Fixed assets are long-term (equipment, buildings) and are depreciated.","Depreciation is a non-cash expense."),
 ("Liquidity","How quickly assets can become cash to pay bills that are due soon.","Profitable companies can still run out of cash. That's why the cash flow statement matters."),
 ("How the statements connect","Net income from the income statement flows into retained earnings on the balance sheet. Ending cash on the cash flow statement equals cash on the balance sheet.","Explaining this link in a roleplay shows comprehensive understanding."),
 ]))

L.append(dict(id="institutions", title="Financial institutions and markets", area="Financial Analysis",
 pis=["FI:336","FI:337","FI:573"],
 why="These three finance-cluster PIs appeared 14 times across the three practice exams.",
 cards=[
 ("Financial institution","An organization that handles financial transactions: banks, credit unions, insurance companies, investment firms.","Their core job is moving money from savers to borrowers (financial intermediation)."),
 ("Commercial bank","For-profit, shareholder-owned bank offering checking, savings and loans. The largest providers of banking services.","Credit unions are not-for-profit and owned by their members."),
 ("Credit union","Not-for-profit, member-owned. Usually lower loan rates and fees than banks.","Deposits insured by the NCUA, not the FDIC."),
 ("Savings and loan (thrift)","Institution focused on savings accounts and home mortgages.","Exam clue: an institution focused on home mortgages is a savings and loan."),
 ("Investment bank","Helps companies and governments raise money by issuing stocks and bonds (underwriting), and advises on mergers.","Investment banks find outside sources of money for firms."),
 ("Insurance company","Sells policies that transfer risk: you pay a premium, they pay covered losses.","Purpose: minimize financial risk."),
 ("Brokerage firm","Buys and sells securities for clients.","SIPC protects customers if the brokerage fails, not against market losses."),
 ("World Bank Group","International institution that provides loans, grants and advice to developing countries to reduce poverty.","The IMF is different: it stabilizes the global monetary system and helps countries in currency crises."),
 ("Federal Reserve","US central bank. Controls the money supply with open market operations, the discount rate and reserve requirements.","Raising rates slows borrowing and inflation. Exams still list these three classic tools; in practice reserve requirements have been 0% since 2020, so the Fed works mainly through its federal funds rate target."),
 ("FDIC","Insures bank deposits up to $250,000 per depositor, per bank, per ownership category.",""),
 ("Financial market","Any organized system for buying and selling financial assets.",""),
 ("Money market","Market for short-term debt that matures within a year: Treasury bills, commercial paper, certificates of deposit.","Treasury bills = money market."),
 ("Capital market","Market for long-term funds: stocks and bonds.",""),
 ("Primary vs secondary market","Primary: a company sells new securities directly to investors (an IPO). Secondary: investors trade those securities with each other afterward (NYSE, Nasdaq).","Trading after the original sale = secondary market."),
 ("Commodities market","Where raw goods like oil, gold, wheat and rubber, and futures contracts on them, are traded.","Oil futures = commodities market."),
 ("Derivatives market","Market for contracts whose value comes from an underlying asset: options, futures, swaps.","Options = derivatives market."),
 ("Insurance market","Market for contracts that protect against losses from catastrophes and crime.",""),
 ("Foreign exchange (forex) market","Where currencies are traded.",""),
 ("Convergence","Financial firms from different sectors merging or offering each other's products, such as a bank merging with an insurance company.","Different sectors = convergence. Same sector = consolidation."),
 ("Consolidation","Firms in the same category merging, such as one retail bank buying another.","Benefits include more liquid capital and economies of scale."),
 ("Bancassurance","A convergence strategy where a bank sells insurance products.","Exam clue: a bank selling insurance to its own customers is bancassurance."),
 ("Fully integrated financial services provider","One firm offering banking, insurance and investments: a financial supermarket.","Made possible by the Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act of 1999."),
 ]))

L.append(dict(id="economy-securities", title="The economy, globalization and reading stock data", area="Financial Analysis",
 pis=["FI:574","FI:575","FI:274","FI:275"],
 why="These four PIs appeared 19 times combined. Stock-table questions are easy points once you know the columns.",
 cards=[
 ("Business cycle","Expansion, peak, contraction (recession), trough.","Heavy investment in new plants and equipment signals producers expect consumer spending to rise."),
 ("Contraction","GDP, hiring and spending decline.","Stock prices tend to fall in contractions."),
 ("Unemployment and markets","High unemployment means lower output and spending, so stock prices usually decrease.",""),
 ("Defensive vs cyclical stocks","Defensive industries (utilities, groceries, healthcare) stay fairly steady through all phases. Cyclical ones (cars, travel) swing with the economy.","Business cycles affect industries differently."),
 ("Interest rates and markets","Higher rates mean bond prices fall and borrowing costs more, which can slow stocks.","Bond prices move opposite to interest rates."),
 ("Inflation","A rise in the general price level that erodes purchasing power. Measured by the CPI.",""),
 ("Bull and bear markets","Bull: prices rising. Bear: prices down 20% or more from a recent high.",""),
 ("Financial globalization","Money and investment flowing freely across borders.","Brings diversification and growth, plus contagion and currency risk."),
 ("Emerging markets","Fast-growing developing economies. High potential growth but more instability and volatility.","Foreign investment can fund a developing country's economic and social development."),
 ("Home bias","Investors' preference for domestic stocks while ignoring the benefits of foreign diversification.","Driven by the higher perceived risk of investing abroad."),
 ("Political events and currency","Markets prefer stability. A surprise election or unrest usually decreases a country's currency value, at least for a while.",""),
 ("Exchange rate","Price of one currency in another. A strong dollar makes imports cheaper and US exports pricier abroad.",""),
 ("Annual report","Yearly report to shareholders with audited financial statements, management's discussion and the auditor's opinion.","One of the most valuable sources of company financial data."),
 ("Form 10-K and 10-Q","SEC filings: the 10-K is the detailed annual report, the 10-Q is quarterly.","Found free on the SEC's EDGAR database."),
 ("Form 8-K","SEC filing that reports major unscheduled events, such as a merger, a CEO leaving or bankruptcy, within a few business days.","Unexpected event = 8-K."),
 ("Prospectus","Document describing a new security offering and its risks.","Companies selling securities must disclose the risks."),
 ("Shareholders' equity","Assets minus liabilities, read from the balance sheet.",""),
 ("Stock table: 52-week high/low","Highest and lowest prices over the past year.","A wide range indicates volatility."),
 ("Stock table: Div and Yld %","Annual dividend per share, and dividend as a percentage of price.","A blank dividend column means the company doesn't currently pay dividends."),
 ("Stock table: Open, Close, Net change","Open: first trade price of the day. Close: last trade price. Net change: close compared with yesterday's close.",""),
 ("Stock table: P/E and volume","P/E: price divided by earnings per share. Volume: number of shares traded that day.",""),
 ("Moving average","Average price over a set number of days, smoothing out daily noise. A rising line means an uptrend.",""),
 ("Relative strength line","Compares a stock to the overall market. Rising means the stock is outperforming the market.","A stock can be rising but falling behind the market."),
 ]))

L.append(dict(id="money-tvm", title="Time value of money, credit and personal finance", area="Financial Analysis",
 pis=["FI:238","FI:062","FI:002","FI:058","FI:059","FI:060","FI:061","FI:065","FI:066","FI:081"],
 why="Expect about 2 math questions from FI:238, and personal finance PIs fill several more.",
 cards=[
 ("Time value of money","A dollar today is worth more than a dollar in the future, because today's dollar can earn interest.",""),
 ("Future value","FV = PV x (1 + i)^n, where i is the interest rate and n is the number of periods.","The interest rate and number of years are the two drivers of growth."),
 ("Present value","PV = FV / (1 + i)^n. How much to invest today to reach a goal.","Compounding finds future value. Discounting finds present value."),
 ("Simple vs compound interest","Simple: interest on the original principal only (P x r x t). Compound: interest on principal plus earned interest.","$1,200 at 10% for 1 year: interest $120, FV $1,320."),
 ("Rule of 72","72 divided by the interest rate is roughly the number of years for money to double.","At 8%, about 9 years."),
 ("Annuity","A series of equal payments at regular intervals, such as loan payments.",""),
 ("Functions of money","Medium of exchange, unit of measure (account) and store of value.","Saving money for later = store of value."),
 ("Forms of financial exchange","Cash, checks, debit, credit, electronic funds transfer, contactless payments and digital wallets.","Contactless purchasing uses cards stored in a digital wallet."),
 ("Cryptocurrency","Digital currency that isn't issued or controlled by any government or bank (decentralized). Transactions are recorded on a blockchain.",""),
 ("Earned vs passive income","Earned: wages, salary, tips. Passive or investment income: interest, dividends, rent, capital gains.","Both are taxed. Earned income also has payroll (FICA) taxes taken out."),
 ("Credit","An arrangement to buy now and pay later.","Builds credit history and enables big purchases, but costs interest."),
 ("Five Cs of credit","Character, capacity, capital, collateral, conditions: how lenders judge a borrower.",""),
 ("Secured vs unsecured loan","Secured loans are backed by collateral. Unsecured loans aren't and charge higher rates.",""),
 ("APR","Annual percentage rate: the yearly cost of borrowing, including fees. Required by the Truth in Lending Act.",""),
 ("Credit score factors","Payment history (35%), amounts owed (30%), length of history (15%), new credit (10%), credit mix (10%).",""),
 ("SMART financial goals","Specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, time-bound.","Example: \"Save $6,000 for an emergency fund by December 2027 by setting aside $250 a month.\""),
 ("Fixed vs variable expenses","Fixed are the same each month (rent). Variable change month to month (groceries, gas).",""),
 ("Net worth","Assets minus liabilities.",""),
 ("Term vs ordinary (whole) life insurance","Term covers a set period, is cheap and has no cash value. Ordinary (whole) life pays on death whenever it happens and builds cash value.",""),
 ("Insurance terms","Premium (what you pay), deductible (paid before coverage starts), beneficiary (who receives the payout), claim (request for payment).",""),
 ]))

L.append(dict(id="managerial", title="Managerial accounting and costs", area="Financial Analysis",
 pis=["FI:658","FI:659","FI:660","FI:661","FI:662","FI:663","FI:106"],
 why="Six finance-cluster PIs with about 16 exam hits combined. Your daily practice roleplays lean on this too.",
 cards=[
 ("Managerial accounting","Preparing financial data for internal users (managers) to plan, control and decide. Not bound by GAAP.","Financial accounting records history for outsiders. Managerial accounting interprets data for decisions."),
 ("What managerial accountants do","Accumulate data on costs and profits, build budgets, run cost and profitability analyses.",""),
 ("Direct cost","Can be traced to one product or project, like materials and production labor.",""),
 ("Indirect cost (overhead)","Shared costs that can't easily be traced to one product, like electricity, rent and IT.","Electricity is the classic indirect cost."),
 ("Fixed vs variable cost","Fixed costs stay the same as volume changes (rent, salaries). Variable costs change with volume (materials, commissions).",""),
 ("Sunk cost","Money already spent that can't be recovered.","Ignore sunk costs when deciding."),
 ("Differential (incremental) cost","The difference in cost between two options.","Option A costs $4,000 and B costs $5,500: differential cost $1,500."),
 ("Opportunity cost","Value of the best alternative you give up.",""),
 ("Marginal analysis","Comparing the added benefit vs the added cost of one more unit or one change in activity.","Keep going while marginal benefit is greater than marginal cost."),
 ("Variance analysis","Comparing planned (budgeted or forecast) results to actual results and finding why they differ.","Use it to spot trends and fix future estimates, like re-estimating labor and time for projects."),
 ("Favorable vs unfavorable variance","Favorable: revenue above budget or costs below. Unfavorable: the reverse.",""),
 ("Cost accounting","Managerial accounting that captures the total cost of production: variable, fixed and operating costs.","Purpose: find the root causes of variances and improve efficiency."),
 ("Cost accounting budgets","Direct materials, direct labor and manufacturing overhead budgets.",""),
 ("Cost allocation","Assigning costs to cost objects like products, departments or regions.",""),
 ("Cost driver","The activity that causes a cost, such as machine hours or square footage, used to allocate overhead.",""),
 ("Budget","A financial plan for a future period that sets targets for income and spending.","The budget is the yardstick for measuring actual results."),
 ("Static vs flexible budget","A static (fixed) budget doesn't adjust for activity levels. A flexible budget adjusts to actual volume.","Weakness of a static budget: no adjustment when activity changes."),
 ("Bottom-up vs top-down budgeting","Bottom-up: department managers build their own budgets, which roll up. Top-down: leadership sets totals.","Bottom-up gives managers ownership of their budget, a good fix for overspending."),
 ("Responsibility centers (bonus)","Cost center, revenue center, profit center, investment center.","Judge managers only on costs they control."),
 ("Cost-volume-profit (bonus)","Contribution margin = price minus variable cost per unit. Break-even units = fixed costs / contribution margin.","Not on your FTDM list, but useful in roleplays."),
 ]))

L.append(dict(id="fim-core", title="Financial-information management: scope, ethics, technology", area="Financial-Information Management",
 pis=["FM:002","FM:003","FM:011"],
 why="Financial-Information Management is 9 questions at regionals, and most competitors never study it.",
 cards=[
 ("Financial-information management (FIM)","Collecting, storing, analyzing and reporting financial data to guide strategic decisions like budget allocation.","Main purpose: accurate reports so managers make sound decisions."),
 ("Benchmarking","Comparing your financial performance with competitors or industry standards.","Identifies weaknesses and opportunities for improvement."),
 ("Mergers and FIM","After a merger, the two companies' systems and data structures often don't match.","Exam clue: mergers often disrupt financial-information systems until the two companies' data is combined."),
 ("Data integrity","Data that is accurate, complete and consistent over its life.",""),
 ("Audit trail","Record of every step of each transaction from start to finish.","Helps identify inconsistencies and fraud."),
 ("Stewardship","Making sure a company's financial resources are used appropriately and ethically to serve its mission.",""),
 ("Conflict of interest","A situation where you can't be truly impartial.","The right move: disclose the connection and remove yourself from the assignment."),
 ("Ethical financial reporting","Honest, transparent reporting builds investor trust and makes raising money easier.",""),
 ("Record retention","Laws require businesses to keep certain financial records for set periods, so they need storage strategies.","The IRS generally says to keep tax records for 3 years, and up to 7 years in some cases."),
 ("Confidentiality","Protecting client and company financial data from unauthorized access.",""),
 ("Automated data collection","Software that captures data automatically, reducing manual entry errors like typos and transposed numbers.",""),
 ("Cloud-based systems","Accessible anywhere and scalable, but they increase exposure to unauthorized access and cyberattacks.","Main downside: cybersecurity breach risk."),
 ("Firewall","Software or hardware that blocks unauthorized users from a network.",""),
 ("Safeguarding data","Security software kept up to date, encryption, multi-factor authentication, access controls and off-site backups.",""),
 ("ERP system","Enterprise resource planning: one integrated system for accounting, inventory, HR and sales.",""),
 ("Accounting software","QuickBooks, Xero and similar tools automate journal entries, invoicing, payroll and reports.",""),
 ("XBRL","Tagging financial data so computers can read it. Used in SEC filings.",""),
 ("AI and machine learning","Used to spot anomalies, detect fraud and forecast.",""),
 ]))

L.append(dict(id="fim-analysis", title="Financial analysis tools, budgeting apps, ratios", area="Financial-Information Management",
 pis=["FM:009","FM:013","FM:014","FM:016"],
 why="18 exam hits. Ratio and analysis-technique questions are very predictable.",
 cards=[
 ("Horizontal (trend) analysis","Comparing the same line item across periods: \"sales rose 8% from last year.\"",""),
 ("Vertical (common-size) analysis","Each line as a percentage of a base: revenue on the income statement, total assets on the balance sheet.","Lets you compare companies of different sizes."),
 ("Ratio analysis","Using relationships between numbers to judge liquidity, profitability, leverage and efficiency.",""),
 ("Current ratio","Current assets / current liabilities. Can the company pay its short-term bills?",""),
 ("Acid-test (quick) ratio","(Current assets minus inventory) / current liabilities.","Can it cover immediate liabilities without selling inventory?"),
 ("Debt-to-equity ratio","Total liabilities / shareholders' equity. How much is financed by debt instead of owners' money.","Used to assess the risk of investing in a company."),
 ("Profitability","Money left after all costs are subtracted from revenue. Net margin = net income / revenue.",""),
 ("Return on assets and return on equity","ROA = net income / total assets. ROE = net income / equity.",""),
 ("Analyzing customer financial info","Credit reports and scores, the five Cs, debt-to-income, payment history and a business customer's ratios.","Businesses set credit standards to decide who gets credit."),
 ("Credit standards","Criteria a business uses to decide which customers to extend credit to.",""),
 ("Budgeting applications","Software to build budgets, compare them to actuals and run what-if scenarios.","Benefit: model unlimited scenarios to see the impact of decisions."),
 ("Cash flow budget","Projects the cash coming in and going out during a period, to spot shortfalls early.",""),
 ("Financial analysis applications","Spreadsheets, databases and software that make analysis faster: ratios, forecasts, dashboards.",""),
 ("Fraud check with data","Comparing employee addresses and phone numbers with vendor records in payables can reveal fake vendors or conflicts of interest.",""),
 ("Spreadsheet functions","SUM, AVERAGE, IF, VLOOKUP/XLOOKUP, PMT, FV, PV, NPV, IRR, pivot tables.",""),
 ("Lean","Maximizing customer value while minimizing waste.",""),
 ("Six Sigma","Data-driven method to reduce variation and defects and improve quality. Steps: DMAIC (define, measure, analyze, improve, control).","Lessens inefficiencies and identifies problem areas."),
 ("Activity-based costing (ABC)","Assigns overhead to products by the activities that actually use it, not by volume.","Gives a more accurate picture of each product's profitability."),
 ("Sustainability reporting","Reporting a company's environmental, social and economic impact (ESG, or the triple bottom line of people, planet, profit).","Can include the costs of managing climate-related risks, putting dollar figures on environmental and social risks."),
 ]))

L.append(dict(id="risk", title="Risk management", area="Risk Management",
 pis=["RM:041","RM:042","RM:043","RM:058","RM:062","SM:075","SM:076"],
 why="Risk Management is 6 questions at regionals and 7 at states, and every PI here was tested 4 to 5 times.",
 cards=[
 ("Risk","The chance of loss or of a different outcome than expected.",""),
 ("Pure vs speculative risk","Pure: only loss is possible (fire). Insurable. Speculative: loss or gain is possible (investing). Not insurable.",""),
 ("Hazard risk","An event or situation that can injure people or damage property or the environment.",""),
 ("Four responses to risk","Avoid it, reduce (control) it, transfer it (insurance, contracts) or retain (accept) it.",""),
 ("Risk management process","Identify risks, assess likelihood and impact, choose a response, implement it, monitor and review.","First step of a risk plan: identify as many known risks as possible."),
 ("Ethics in risk management","Honestly disclosing risks and not shifting them to customers by deception.","Hiding safety flaws in fine print is unethical risk management."),
 ("Ethical risk","The risk that a company can't live up to its own code of ethics, plus losses and reputation damage from cutting corners like using poor-quality materials.",""),
 ("Technology in risk management","Processes huge amounts of data to quantify risk and run predictive models.","Machine learning can flag unusual transactions that humans might miss."),
 ("Risk modeling","Simulating possible outcomes using historical data, probabilities and expert predictions.",""),
 ("Backups","Regular backups stored off-site limit losses from outages, fire or cyberattacks.",""),
 ("Legal side of risk management","Staying compliant with laws, using contracts to assign responsibility, following OSHA safety rules and insurance regulations.",""),
 ("Sarbanes-Oxley Act (2002)","Requires public companies to establish, maintain and report on internal controls over financial reporting. CEOs and CFOs certify the statements.",""),
 ("Captive insurance","An insurance company set up and owned by a parent company to insure the parent's own risks.",""),
 ("Risk control","Actions that reduce how often or how badly losses happen.","Example: implementing and monitoring safety policies."),
 ("Internal controls","Policies and procedures that prevent and detect errors and fraud.","Example: two employees must sign any transaction over $10,000."),
 ("Separation (segregation) of duties","Dividing tasks so no one person controls a whole transaction, such as approving, paying and recording.",""),
 ("External (uncontrollable) risks","Risks from outside the company that it can't control, such as inflation, tax increases, recessions and natural disasters.","Tax increases are uncontrollable and uninsurable."),
 ("Enterprise risk management (ERM)","Managing all risks across the whole organization in one integrated framework, not department by department.","Weakness: relies heavily on management's estimates and inputs."),
 ("Risk inventory (register)","A list of all of an organization's critical, enterprise-wide risks.",""),
 ("Strategic vs operational risk","Strategic: risk from big decisions and long-term goals, like product positioning and competition. Operational: risk from daily processes, people and systems failing.",""),
 ]))

L.append(dict(id="law", title="Business law and compliance in finance", area="Business Law",
 pis=["BL:133","BL:134","BL:148","BL:149","BL:002","BL:003","BL:072"],
 why="Business Law is 7 questions at regionals. The fraud-scheme terms are guaranteed points.",
 cards=[
 ("Fiduciary","Someone legally required to act in another person's best interest, like a financial advisor or a board of directors.",""),
 ("Insider trading","Trading a company's stock using material, non-public information. Illegal.",""),
 ("Front-running","A broker or analyst trades ahead of a big client's pending order to profit from the price move.",""),
 ("Pump and dump","Hyping a cheap stock (often online) to push its price up, then selling before it crashes.",""),
 ("Ponzi scheme","Paying earlier investors with new investors' money instead of real profits.",""),
 ("Pyramid scheme","Members make money mainly by recruiting new members, and money funnels up to a few at the top.",""),
 ("Affinity fraud","A scammer targets members of a group they belong to or claim to belong to, like a church or community.",""),
 ("Securities laws","Securities Act of 1933 (register and disclose new securities). Securities Exchange Act of 1934 (created the SEC, regulates trading).",""),
 ("Other finance laws","Gramm-Leach-Bliley (financial privacy and consolidation), Sarbanes-Oxley (internal controls), Dodd-Frank (after 2008, created the CFPB), Bank Secrecy Act and USA PATRIOT Act (anti-money laundering).",""),
 ("Capital gains and losses","Profit or loss from selling an investment. Losses can offset gains for tax purposes.","Short-term gains (1 year or less) are taxed as ordinary income. Long-term gains get lower rates."),
 ("Luxury tax","Tax on nonessential, expensive goods like high-end cars and jewelry.",""),
 ("Estate tax","Tax on the value of assets transferred at death.","Property passing directly to a surviving spouse is generally exempt."),
 ("Gift and charitable donations","Donations to qualified charities can be tax-deductible.",""),
 ("Debt vs equity and taxes","Interest paid on debt is tax-deductible; dividends aren't. That encourages companies to use debt.",""),
 ("Compliance","Following laws plus internal and external regulations.","Builds public trust and keeps financial institutions stable."),
 ("Compliance department","Its job is preventing violations, then detecting and correcting them.","Prevention comes first."),
 ("Noncompliance consequences","Fines, audits, lawsuits and a damaged reputation.",""),
 ("Regtech (compliance technology)","Automates monitoring, reporting and record-keeping.","Reduces compliance costs and identifies patterns and trends."),
 ("Contract elements","Offer, acceptance, consideration (something of value exchanged), capacity, legality.",""),
 ("Agent and principal","An agent acts for the principal and must act in the principal's best interest.",""),
 ("Forms of ownership","Sole proprietorship, partnership, corporation, S corp, LLC, nonprofit (primary goal is a mission, not profit).",""),
 ]))
